          "solution": {
            "text": "<p>From the figure, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi></math>-component remains unchanged while the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Y</mi></math>-component is reverse. Then, the velocity at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn><mover><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">j</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math>.</p>",
